ly a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+3I:$\n\n$$\nA+3I=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+3I:$\n\n$$\n(A+3I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+3I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152384 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152384 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152384 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152384 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152384 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6f4f929d-c8e6-554e-bc37-364f30077a9f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152384 #edb/ref 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+5I:$\n\n$$\nA+5I=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)w=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Again, since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $x$ under the action of $A+5I:$\n\n$$\n(A+5I)x=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{v}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{w}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}\\overset{A+5I\\,}{\\to }{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+5I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $3$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-5$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153168 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153168 #edb/ref 1061 33794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153168 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "70c56d2e-edef-5c66-b434-a79d78754fe4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153168 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153169 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153169 #edb/ref 1061 33795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153169 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ba3a9ff-1652-5e2f-a17b-31f53f5fa7ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153169 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152224 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153170 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153170 #edb/ref 1061 33799 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153170 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4c7afd25-604b-599f-93ac-e074a6562587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153170 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153171 #edb/ref 1061 33800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153171 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c580f07b-112f-562a-a89e-5d7287e732f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153171 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153172 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153172 #edb/ref 1061 34485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153172 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3ec840df-f049-5126-988c-9c7bbb99172d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153172 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153411 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153173 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153173 #edb/ref 1061 34486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153173 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cd7d895d-dd6d-5fb6-acf9-cde71c0edb5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153173 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153410 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $A$ be an  $m \\times m$ matrix. Recall that a nonzero vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ if\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}_1 = \\lambda \\mathbf{v}_1.\n$$\n\nWe can write this equation as \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{v}_1 = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ maps the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ to the zero vector $\\mathbf{0}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nNow suppose that there exists a nonzero vector $\\mathbf v_2$ that is mapped to the zero vector in *two* steps by the matrix $A-\\lambda I.$ That is,\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf v_2 = \\mathbf v_1 \\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad (A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf v_1 = \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_1$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda.$\n\nWe can represent this schematically as a chain of mappings, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I} \\mathbf{v}_{1} \\xrightarrow{A- \\lambda I}  \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nWe call $\\mathbf v_2$ a **generalized eigenvector of rank** $2$ because it is mapped to the zero vector in two steps. \n\nBy the same idea, an eigenvector of $A$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ because it is mapped to the zero vector in one step." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153410 #edb/ref 1065 5419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153410 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "88994c46-d16a-531c-895a-e2c3fdddfc1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153410 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" 