d{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A+4I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247011 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247011 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247011 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247011 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nII. $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$\n\nIII. $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of $A$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247011 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd32b406-fe2e-5167-ace1-4082f15020f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247011 #edb/ref 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A+2I:$\n\n$$\nA+2I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A+2I:$\n\n$$\n(A+2I)v=[\\begi